# 1. Distributed web infrastructure

## The flow

The user resolves www.foobar.com through DNS, which now points to the load
balancer's IP. The browser opens a TCP connection to the load balancer on port
80. HAproxy picks one of the two servers and forwards the request. On that
server, Nginx serves the static assets or hands the request to the application
server, which runs the code base and queries MySQL. The response travels back
through HAproxy to the browser.

## Why each element was added

**1 load balancer (HAproxy)**
It gives the infrastructure a single entry point while spreading the traffic
across several servers. It removes the web/application tier as a single point
of failure and lets us add capacity by adding servers instead of buying a
bigger one.

**A second server**
Two servers mean roughly twice the capacity, and the site survives the loss of
one of them. It also makes rolling deployments possible: take one out of the
pool, update it, put it back, then do the other - with no downtime.

**MySQL Primary-Replica cluster**
Instead of two unrelated databases, the two MySQL instances are set up as one
cluster so both servers see the same data. The replica also gives us a warm
copy for backups and read scaling.

## Specifics

**What distribution algorithm the load balancer is configured with**
Round Robin. HAproxy keeps a list of the healthy backend servers and sends
each new request to the next server in the list, cycling back to the start at
the end. Request 1 goes to server 1, request 2 to server 2, request 3 to
server 1 again, and so on. It is simple and spreads traffic evenly when the
servers have similar capacity. HAproxy also health-checks the backends and
stops sending traffic to one that stops answering.

**Active-Active or Active-Passive**
This setup is Active-Active: both servers are in the pool and both receive
traffic at the same time.

In an Active-Passive setup only one node serves traffic while the other sits
idle as a standby, taking over only when the active node fails. Active-Passive
is simpler and avoids data-consistency problems, but half the hardware does
nothing most of the time. Active-Active uses all the capacity and degrades
gracefully, but every node must be able to handle any request.

**How a database Primary-Replica (Master-Slave) cluster works**
The Primary is the only node that accepts writes. Every change it makes is
written to a binary log. Each Replica connects to the Primary, reads that log,
and replays the same changes on its own copy of the data. Replication is
usually asynchronous, so a Replica can be a fraction of a second behind.

**Difference between the Primary node and the Replica node regarding the
application**
The application sends all writes - INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE - to the Primary
only. It can send reads - SELECT - to either, and sending them to the Replica
takes load off the Primary. The application must tolerate the small
replication lag: data just written to the Primary may not be readable from the
Replica for a moment.

## Issues with this infrastructure

**Where are the SPOFs**
- The load balancer. There is only one, so if HAproxy goes down nothing
  reaches the servers at all.
- The MySQL Primary. Only it accepts writes, so if it dies the site becomes
  read-only until a replica is promoted.

**Security issues**
- No firewall, so every port on every machine is reachable from the internet.
- No HTTPS, so traffic travels in clear text and can be read or modified in
  transit. Passwords and session cookies are exposed.

**No monitoring**
Nothing is collecting metrics or logs. We only learn the site is down when a
user complains, we cannot see a problem building up (disk filling, memory
leaking, response time creeping), and we have no data to debug an incident
after the fact.
